Vocal Cord Nodules/Polyps
Opthalmology & ENT
Chronic hoarseness/breathiness, Voice fatigue, Vocal fold lesions on laryngoscopy

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OET-050
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of chronic hoarseness leading to sudden, near-total loss of voice and severe anxiety regarding laryngeal cancer.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported an older adult with a long-standing smoking history who experienced a sudden, severe worsening of chronic hoarseness, now presenting with near-total voice loss and extreme psychological distress. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to rule out an acute airway obstruction from a rapidly expanding laryngeal mass, evaluate for acute stridor, and provide immediate clinical staging and psychological relief prior to routine services.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My voice has been gravelly for months, but tonight it just disappeared into a squeak. I am terrified I have throat cancer."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports chronic, progressive hoarseness, breathiness, and a raspy voice quality over the last 8 months. Endorses frequent vocal fatigue that noticeably worsens toward the end of the day or after speaking. Notes recent pitch breaks, reduced vocal range, and an inability to project their voice. Strong history of demanding professional voice use (auctioneer) and a 20 pack-year smoking history. Reports a constant sensation of a lump or foreign body in the throat, causing constant throat clearing. Tonight, the voice functionally failed after a long shift. Denies hemoptysis, weight loss, or dysphagia.
Functional Status: Highly distressed, attempting to force speech resulting in a strained whisper.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe anxiety regarding malignancy.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/90 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 85 Kg
General: Alert, extremely anxious, visibly straining the anterior neck musculature to produce sound.
Physical Exam: Respiratory exam is completely normal; absolutely no stridor, wheeze, or prolonged expiratory phase. Neck examination reveals a midline trachea, no palpable thyroid nodules, and most importantly, no hard, fixed cervical lymphadenopathy. Oropharynx is clear. Evaluation of phonation reveals incomplete glottic closure resulting in a severely breathy, weak sound. Absolute absence of systemic inflammatory symptoms, fever, or severe airway obstruction.
Environmental Observations: Patient pacing the room; spouse attempting to calm them.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Suspected Vocal Cord Polyp/Nodules - Status: Acute functional decompensation
Differentials:

Laryngeal Squamous Cell Carcinoma - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The specific trigger of functional failure following heavy voice use, the absence of fixed cervical lymphadenopathy, absence of hemoptysis, and lack of systemic weight loss. However, given the smoking history, formal fast-track ENT scoping is absolutely mandatory to definitively exclude.

Acute Viral Laryngitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The 8-month chronic, progressive history of symptoms prior to this acute failure, and the complete lack of acute viral prodrome or fever.

Vocal Cord Paralysis (e.g., recurrent laryngeal nerve lesion) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The symptoms being closely tied to vocal fatigue and voice abuse throughout the day, rather than a constant, sudden, and unchanging breathy dysphonia regardless of use.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (High anxiety, mandatory cancer exclusion required)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented in a state of acute crisis due to the sudden functional failure of a chronically impaired voice, heavily shadowed by the fear of malignancy due to their smoking history. Immediate physical evaluation was critical to establish airway safety and rule out impending gross obstruction or massive metastatic cervical disease. By confirming a patent airway and the absence of ominous external neck signs, the urgent visit successfully de-escalated the immediate panic. The clinical picture strongly points to a benign mucosal lesion (polyp/nodule) exacerbated by overuse. However, the visit critically facilitated immediate, enforced voice rest and the generation of an urgent, fast-track ENT referral to definitively exclude malignancy.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Imposed strict, absolute vocal rest. Generated an urgent "Fast-Track" referral to the local ENT/Head and Neck clinic for flexible videostroboscopy to definitively visualize the vocal folds and rule out malignancy.
Positive Behavioral Change: To facilitate strict vocal rest and reduce anxiety, the patient must download a text-to-speech application on their smartphone immediately and place their phone on a lanyard around their neck. The instant ability to communicate complex thoughts without physically straining the vocal cords removes the frustration of silence (positive reinforcement), ensuring total compliance with the vocal rest necessary to reduce the acute swelling before the specialist scope.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: GP review in 3 days to ensure the urgent ENT referral has been received and triaged.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the patient develops noisy, high-pitched breathing (stridor), feels they cannot get enough air into their lungs, or starts coughing up fresh blood.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: A camera test (laryngoscopy) by a specialist is absolutely required to formally diagnose the cause of your hoarseness.
Clinical Course: Your voice has failed due to exhaustion of the vocal muscles working against a likely benign swelling. With strict silence, some functional voice will return in a few days, but the underlying problem requires specialist assessment.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

If your breathing suddenly becomes noisy or difficult, or you begin coughing up blood, call an ambulance immediately.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Years of heavy voice use and smoking have likely caused a blister or polyp to form on your vocal cord, preventing it from closing properly. Tonight, the muscles simply gave out. You must rest completely so the swelling goes down enough for the specialist to get a clear look with their camera.
Actionable Steps: Stop smoking immediately. Do not speak or whisper at all; use the text-to-speech app on your phone. You must attend the urgent ENT appointment to formally check the vocal cords.

